Enclosures/evidence (BayHStA, Imperial Chamber Court):
The predecessor file (Q 8a/10) contains:
- Volume I: agreement of Bishop Melchior of Würzburg with Count Ludwig von Stolberg concerning the Würzburg prince-bishopric fiefs of Count Michael III von Wertheim, 1556 (fol. 129r ff.); feudal reversals by Count Ludwig von Stolberg, 1556, and by Count Dietrich von Manderscheid as feudal holder for his sister-in-law, Countess Katharina von Eberstein, 1575 (fol. 145r ff., 206v ff.); fief charter of Bishop Friedrich of Würzburg for Count Ludwig von Stolberg, 1561 (fol. 150v ff.); instruction of Emperor Maximilian II on what his councillor Georg Ludwig von Seinsheim should undertake in Würzburg on behalf of Count Ludwig von Stolberg, 1570 (fol. 162v ff.); deed of renunciation by the widowed Countess Katharina von Wertheim of the inheritance that had fallen to her, on the death of her daughter Countess Barbara von Wertheim (from her marriage to Count Michael III von Wertheim), in favor of her father Count Ludwig von Stolberg, 1561 (fol. 222r ff.); fief charter of Emperor Rudolf II to Counts Philipp von Eberstein, Dietrich von Manderscheid, and Ludwig von Löwenstein for the County of Wertheim, 1579 (fol. 266v ff.); judgment deed of the Imperial Provincial Court of the Burgraviate of Nuremberg on the suit brought by Wilhelm von Werdenau, house commander at Nuremberg, in place of the Teutonic Order’s Deutschmeister Eberhard von Seinsheim against Count Johann II von Wertheim concerning the escort belonging to the estate and lordship of Prozelten, 1429 (fol. 270v ff.); will of Count Johann I von Wertheim, 1398 (fol. 346v ff.); confirmation deed of Emperor Maximilian II, 1575, concerning Count Ludwig von Stolberg’s disposition in favor of his sons-in-law, 1566 (fol. 353r ff.);
- Volume II (with a parchment cover leaf from an Apocalypse manuscript): witness statements before an arbitral court commission, 1584 (fol. 434r ff.); fief charters of Kings and Emperors Karl IV, Ruprecht, Sigismund, Friedrich III, Maximilian I, Karl V, and Maximilian II to Counts Eberhard, Johann I, Johann II, Georg I, Wilhelm, Johann III, Michael II, and Michael III von Wertheim, Ludwig von Stolberg, Philipp von Eberstein, Dietrich von Manderscheid, and Ludwig von Löwenstein for the County of Wertheim, 1362–1575 (fol. 509r ff., 521r ff.); exemption privilege of King Sigismund for Counts Johann II and Michael I von Wertheim, 1422 (fol. 511v ff.); deeds concerning the sale of the estate and other properties at Kirschfurt by Abbot Ludwig II of Amorbach to the County of Wertheim, 1273; by Count Rudolf II von Wertheim to the collegiate foundation of St. Peter and Alexander at Aschaffenburg, 1285; and by Dean Stephan (Anhalt) and the chapter at Aschaffenburg to Count Johann III von Wertheim, 1478 (fol. 514r ff.); deed of sale by Johann Schonbrot, vicar of the altar of St. John at the foundation in Aschaffenburg, to Count Johann III von Wertheim for revenues at Kirschfurt, 1479 (fol. 518r f.); deed of sale by Philipp von Riedern to Count Johann III von Wertheim for half the village of Boxtal (here: Boxell), 1489 (fol. 519r ff.); certificate of testimony concerning the escort from Esselbach (here: Espelbach) to Oberzell (here: Herrenzell), 1422 (fol. 550v f.); judgment deed of the cent court at Wertheim, 1422 (fol. 551v ff.); extracts from customs registers concerning customs revenue at Wertheim and Freudenberg, 1519–1584 (fol. 553r ff.); petitions for passage free of customs, 1579–1582 (fol. 602v ff.); award deed of Elector Philipp of the Palatinate in the dispute between Elector Dieter of Mainz and Count Johann III von Wertheim over Külsheim, 1480 (fol. 608v ff.); arbitration award of an arbitral tribunal chaired by Wolf Kämmerer von Worms, called von Dalberg, in the dispute between Elector Albrecht II of Mainz and Count Michael II von Wertheim over escort, grazing, fishing, and other rights, 1527 (fol. 636r ff.); charter of King Johann of Bohemia to Count Rudolf IV von Wertheim for 400 Brabant pounds in connection with Königheim Castle being granted as a fief, 1329 (fol. 654r); fief charters of Emperor Karl IV and Kings Sigismund, Ladislaus, Georg, Wladislaw II, and Ferdinand I of Bohemia to Counts Eberhard, Georg I, Johann II, Johann III, Georg II, Michael II, and Michael III von Wertheim for Wertheim Castle and town and Kreuzwertheim, 1362–1532 (fol. 654v ff., 657r ff.); deed of Emperor Karl IV to Count Eberhard von Wertheim concerning conversion of the count’s court at Wertheim into allodial property, 1366 (fol. 656r f.); fief charters of Bishops Johann I, Rudolf II, Lorenz, Konrad II, and Melchior of Würzburg to Counts Johann I, Wilhelm, Johann III, Michael II, Erasmus, and Michael III von Wertheim for the hereditary office of chamberlain, Freudenberg Castle and town, Schweinberg Castle, the castles of Laudenbach and Remlingen, and half the village of Lengfurt, 1401–1546 (fol. 670v ff.); deed of sale by Hans Bosse zu Fritzdorf in the County of Neuenahr to Count Johann III von Wertheim for an estate at Remlingen, 1482 (fol. 686v f.); deed of sale by Count Poppo IX of Henneberg to his brother-in-law Count Ludwig V of Rieneck for revenues at Tiefenthal and Duttenbrunn, 1332 (fol. 687v f.); deed of sale by Wilhelm von Elma to Count Johann II von Wertheim for properties, revenues, and tithe shares at Holzkirchhausen (here: Hausen), Dertingen (usually Derdingen in the file), Heidenfeld, Lengfurt, Karbach, Birkenfeld, Zimmern, Hafenlohr, Rothenfels, Grünfeld, Urspringen, Steinfeld, Rohrbach, Wiesenfeld, Niederhofen, Laudenbach (here: Luttenbach), Karlstadt, Harrbach, Wernfeld, Billingshausen, and elsewhere, 1431 (fol. 688v ff.); deed of sale by the spouses Sibott and Heilig Hofschultheiß to Rudolf von Elma for revenues at Urspringen, 1388 (fol. 696r f.); deed of sale by Agnes von Wertheim, as abbess, and the convent at Unterzell (here: Nidern Celle; also Frauenzell) to Count Johann I von Wertheim for an annual revenue at Uettingen, 1395 (fol. 697r f.); deed of sale by Heinrich and Eberhard Zieche to Count Johann II von Wertheim for a meadow at Heidenfeld, 1410 (fol. 698r f.); deed of sale by Reinhard III von Hanau and his wife Katharina von Nassau to Counts Johann II von Wertheim and Thomas II von Rieneck for properties and revenues at Greußenheim, Birkenfeld, Altertheim, Karbach, Billingshausen, Urspringen, Steinfeld, Leinach (here: Linach), Rohrbach, Duttenbrunn, and Erlabrunn (?), 1414 (fol. 699r ff.); deed of sale by Götz Schetzlein zu Uettingen to Count Johann II von Wertheim for a meadow at Uettingen, 1421 (fol. 701r ff.); deed of sale by Johann von Buchenau and the convent at Holzkirchen to Hans and Margaretha von Dottenheim for the great tithe at Neubrunn, 1428 (fol. 702v ff.); deed of sale by Dieter Rüdt zu Bödigheim to Hans Wintersbach for an estate at Erlenbach, 1482 (fol. 705r ff.); deed of sale by Apollonia Lochinger, widow of Philipp von Hettersdorf, to Count Michael II von Wertheim for one third of the small tithe at Holzkirchhausen, 1509 (fol. 707v ff.); deed of sale by Ludwig von Hanau, canon of Würzburg, to Konrad Fuchsstatt, provost of Triefenstein, for revenues at Greußenheim, Birkenfeld, Rohrbach, Erlabrunn, and Oberaltertheim, 1379 (fol. 709r ff.); deed of sale by Heinrich Schlemper von Hardheim to Count Eberhard von Wertheim for half the village of Lengfurt, 1357 (fol. 712v f.); deed of sale by Count Poppo IX of Henneberg to Count Rudolf IV von Wertheim for Unteraltertheim (here: Nidernalterheim), 1338 (fol. 713v ff.); certificate of testimony of the Britter court at Holzkirchen, 1406 (fol. 715v ff.); agreement between the community of Erlenbach and Count Johann II von Wertheim concerning protection and services, 1409 (fol. 719r ff.); deed of sale by Georg, Jost, and Hans von Riedern to Count Michael I von Wertheim for their estate at Wolferstetten, 1429 (fol. 724r ff.); deed of sale by Lisa von Laudenbach, widow of Georg von Riedern, and her sons Konrad, Peter, and Heinrich von Riedern to Count Georg I von Wertheim for the village of Steinbach, 1430 (fol. 725v ff.); deed of sale by Hans von Rechenberg and his wife Anna von Rosenberg to Count Michael II von Wertheim for a wood near Brehmen, 1434 (fol. 728r ff.); deeds of sale by Georg von Hartheim to Count Wilhelm von Wertheim for his revenues at Hardheim, 1466, and for his estate Betzwiesen near Schweinberg, 1480 (fol. 729r ff.); deed of sale by Fritz Hund to Count Erasmus von Wertheim for shares in the wine tithe at Paimar (here: Bewmer), 1493 (fol. 734r ff.); deed of sale by the spouses Egen and Grete Seemann to Count Eberhard von Wertheim for properties and revenues at Hardheim, 1367 (fol. 737r ff.); deed of sale by Jakob Staud, steward at Miltenberg, to Count Wilhelm von Wertheim for properties and revenues at Königheim (here: Könnigken) and Pülfringen (here: Bülfericken), 1455 (fol. 740v ff.); deed of sale by Abbot Konrad IV and the convent at Bronnbach to Count Wilhelm von Wertheim for properties at Pülfringen, 1477 (fol. 743r ff.); deed of sale and renunciation by the spouses Konrad and Anna During to Countess Katharina von Wertheim for their properties at Pülfringen, 1372 (fol. 744v ff.); deed of sale by Provost Konrad Fuchsstatt and the chapter at Triefenstein to Count Johann I von Wertheim for an annual revenue at Pülfringen, 1393 (fol. 747v ff.); inheritance charter by Fritz Stumpf to Hans Hamer for the Klimpfenhube at Schweinberg, 1419 (fol. 749r ff.); feudal reversals concerning shares in the Lower Castle at Hardheim, held as a fief from the County of Wertheim, by Kunz von Hartheim, 1440, Horneck the Younger von Hornberg, 1448, and Wernher von Hartheim, 1457 (fol. 751r ff.); deed of sale by Eitel Fuchsstatt to Count Michael III von Wertheim for his castle at Laudenbach, 1533 (fol. 755v ff.); pledge and sale deed by Konrad Fuchsstatt to Count Michael II von Wertheim for revenues at Laudenbach, 1504 (fol. 759r ff., 771r ff.); deed of sale by Wilhelm Diemar zu Wiesenfeld to Count Michael III von Wertheim for properties and revenues at Laudenbach, 1545 (fol. 762r ff.); deeds of sale by Eitel Voit von Rieneck to Count Johann III von Wertheim for properties and revenues at Laudenbach, Steinfeld, Harrbach, and Wiesenfeld, and at Rothenfels, Zimmern, Roden, Altfeld, and Oberwittbach (here: Wittbach), 1472 (fol. 765r ff., 772v ff.); deed of sale by Hans von Ostheim to Count Johann I von Wertheim for properties in and around Laudenbach, 1390 (fol. 767v ff.); certificate of testimony by the priors of the Dominican, Augustinian, and Carmelite monasteries and the guardian of the Franciscan monastery at Würzburg concerning a grain rent charged on the mill at Laudenbach, 1449 (fol. 769v ff.); transfer deed by Countess Amalia von Neuenahr, née Countess von Wertheim, in favor of her great-nephew Count Michael III von Wertheim, subject to her right of reversion, 1532, and confirmation deed of Emperor Karl V, 1532 (fol. 774v ff.); assignment deed by Electress Amalia of the Palatinate and her brother Count Adolf von Neuenahr in favor of Count Ludwig von Löwenstein, 1576 (fol. 795r ff.);
Printed mandate of Emperor Rudolf II ordering suspension of publication of the Imperial Aulic Council mandates issued in the dispute between the plaintiff count and Baron Wilhelm von Criechingen, 1601 (Q 19);
statement of Würzburg prince-bishopric litigation costs (Q 36);
Imperial Aulic Council judgment in the dispute between the plaintiff count and Baron Wilhelm von Criechingen, 1605 (Q 43);
undated, itemized petition by the plaintiff asking the Imperial Aulic Council to punish the opposing party for breach of the public peace (probably part of the plaintiff’s petition for restitution = Q 46);
further enclosures to the plaintiff’s petition for restitution (Q 61): will of Countess Katharina von Eberstein, 1598 (Lit. A); undated deed of renunciation by Countess Anna von Stolberg on the occasion of her marriage to the plaintiff count (Lit. B); agreement of Prince-Abbot Wolfgang of Fulda with Count Ludwig von Stolberg concerning the provostship of Holzkirchen, 1564 (Lit. G); feudal reversals by Hans and Schweikhard von Dürn concerning Reichartshausen and other comital Wertheim fiefs, 1445 and 1570; by Friedrich von Riedern concerning Holzkirchhausen, 1407; by Heinz Rüdt von Collenberg concerning Trembach, 1415; by Fritz Truchseß (von Baldersheim), Konrad von Finsterlohr, and the guardianship of Georg Sigmund Truchseß von Baldersheim concerning shares in the grain and wine tithes at Dainbach, 1405, 1453, and 1602; and by Andreas and Jost von Riedern concerning tithe shares at Wolferstetten, Weckbach, and Steinbach, 1457 and 1549 (Lit. I–M, O–Q, Sss, Ttt); debt instrument by Eberhard Rüdt von Collenberg to his mother Anna von Thüngen for 3,000 florins, 1421 (Lit. N); judgment of the comital feudal court at Wertheim on Count Johann II von Wertheim’s suit against Heinz Rüdt von Collenberg concerning Hasloch, Hasselberg, and Trembach, 1415 (Lit. R); privilege of Emperor Karl IV for Count Johann I von Wertheim concerning his own court, stocks, and gallows at Freudenberg, 1372 (Lit. T); settlement proposals by Elector Philipp of the Palatinate as mediator in Würzburg–Wertheim negotiations, 1502 (Lit. X); genealogical table of the descendants of Count Wilhelm von Wertheim (Lit. a); receipt by Electress Amalia of the Palatinate, née Countess von Neuenahr, for payment of 6,000 florins by the plaintiff count, 1578 (Lit. c); letter of protection by Pope Hadrian IV for Bronnbach Monastery, 1159 (Lit. g); deed of Archbishop Gerlach of Mainz concerning Abbot Billung of Bronnbach’s claim for compensation for damage inflicted on the monastery by Külsheim and Gamburg, 1354 (Li